{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EFYAR7SXXsBoHbuqtdSg44","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction: Pairwise Confusability, Merge Amplification, and the Limits of Decomposition\"\n\nThis 9-page paper studies why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas. It presents three contributions:\n1. A multi-channel degradation model: type count causes recall decline (attention dilution), type confusability causes type confusion errors, and these channels combine additively (2×2 ANOVA, interaction p=0.14).\n2. Pairwise embedding-based Schema Confusability Scores (SCS) predict empirical type confusion (ρ=0.35, p<10⁻⁴), validated across two models (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B) and two embeddings (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n3. Schema DAG Decomposition (SDGD) consistently fails to outperform single-pass extraction. The root cause is \"merge amplification\"—compounding deduplication errors when reassembling batch outputs. Even SDGD v2 with merge mitigation achieves only −2.5 F1 vs single-pass at 5.8× latency.\n\nTasks: Few-NERD (NER, 66 types), DocRED (RE, 96 types), MAVEN (event detection, 168 types).\nModels: Qwen3-32B-Instruct (primary), LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct (validation).\nEmbeddings: Qwen3-32B, MiniLM-L6-v2.\n\n## Key Tables and Results\n\n**Table 1 (SCS validation):** Qwen3 embeddings ρ=0.35 (p=10⁻⁴), MiniLM ρ=0.28 (p=10⁻³), Top-10 overlap 5/10 and 4/10.\n\n**Table 2:** Top 5 confused pairs. Intra-domain pairs well-predicted by SCS; cross-domain pair (location-GPE↔location-bodiesofwater) at SCS rank 18 shows limitation.\n\n**Table 3 (Cross-model):** LLaMA pairwise r=0.08 (p=4×10⁻⁴), per-type ρ=−0.18 (p=0.08). About half the effect size of Qwen3.\n\n**Table 4-5 (2×2 factorial ANOVA):** Count F=8.7 p=0.004, BCS F=10.6 p=0.002, interaction F=1.7 p=0.14. Low count/low BCS F1=0.38, high count/high BCS F1=0.21.\n\n**Table 6-7 (DocRED):** Count curve 52%→18% (1→96 types). BCS-controlled delta −14.5pp at count 8, −18.3pp at count 16. Pairwise r=0.09 (p=0.003).\n\n**Table 8 (Few-NERD decomposition):** Single-pass F1=0.445 > SDGD v2=0.420 > Random-batch=0.315 > SDGD-cal=0.300 > Per-type=0.292 > SDGD-zero=0.251. Merge dup rates: per-type 38%, random-batch 35%, SDGD v2 12%.\n\n**Table 9 (MAVEN decomposition):** Single-pass relaxed F1=0.245 > SDGD-cal=Random-batch=0.190 > SDGD-zero=0.170 > Per-type=0.150.\n\n**Table 10 (LLaMA count curve):** Non-monotonic pattern, F1 recovers at intermediate counts (0.31→0.35→0.33). BCS-controlled effect at count 12: −6.0 F1 (partial r=0.32, p=0.04).\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes:\n- exp-005: DocRED Confusability Study (count curve + BCS correlation, 96 types)\n- exp-006: Interaction Effect 2×2 factorial on Few-NERD\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN (168 types)\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (8 calibration examples)\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN\n- exp-012: LLaMA Multi-Model Validation\n- exp-013: Random Batching ablation (10 seeds)\n- exp-014: Per-Type Splitting ablation\n- exp-015: SCS Validation (rank correlation embedding vs empirical)\n- exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd: SDGD v2 with merge mitigation\n\nAll appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on **methodology** and **experiments** using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Address:\n\n1. **Experimental design quality**: Are the controlled experiments (count curves, BCS-controlled comparisons, 2×2 factorial, decomposition comparisons) well-designed? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Sample sizes adequate? Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, prompts, evaluation metrics specified?\n4. **Completeness**: Are there missing experiments or ablations that would strengthen the claims? Compare against the experiment design reference.\n5. **Evaluation metrics**: Are the metrics appropriate for each task? Any concerns about relaxed F1, accuracy choices?\n6. **Methodological soundness of SDGD**: Is the graph coloring approach well-motivated? Are the variants (zero, cal, v2) properly ablated?\n7. **Weaknesses in experimental execution**: Any red flags in the numbers (e.g., LLaMA non-monotonic count curve, random > SDGD-zero)?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing experiments or analyses\n- Detailed scores: Soundness, Empirical Quality, Reproducibility, Overall (1-6 scale)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is an analysis/empirical paper, not a methods paper proposing a new SOTA system.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1421,"cache_read_input_tokens":46559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1421},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"3d15632e-f7ef-4866-afbd-692ba1026f40"}
