{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AM2SfXQvrVS4t8Y5aQE1F5","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"# 项目 schema-dag-extraction 概况\\n\\n## 实验状态 (14 个)\\n\\n- **exp012_llama_fewnerd_r2**: RUNNING — \\n- **exp012_llama_fewnerd**: DONE (failed) — \\n- **exp007_error_taxonomy**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp_fewrel_formal_80rel**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd**: PREPARING — \\n- **exp_fewrel_formal**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd_r2**: DONE (negative) — \\n- **exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd**: DONE (failed) — \\n- **fewrel_pilot**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp004_maven_bcs**: DONE (negative) — \\n- **exp002_confusability_controlled**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp003a_prompt_v2_rerun**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp-004**: DONE (success) — \\n- **exp001_count_baseline**: DONE (success) — \\n\\n## 最新报告\\n# Handover — 2026-05-15\\n\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\n\\n## 运行中\\n| exp_id | 假设 | 状态 | 关键指标 | W&B |\\n|--------|------|------|---------|-----|\\n| exp012_llama_fewnerd_r2 | — | — | — | — |\\n\\n## 待处理\\n- exp008_sdgd_v2_fewnerd（准备中）\\n\\n## 近期结论\\n- **exp012_llama_fewnerd** (failed): —\\n- **exp007_error_taxonomy** (success): —\\n- **exp_fewrel_formal_80rel** (success): —\\n\\n## 趋势\\n成功 8 / 负向 2 / 失败 2 / 共 12\\n\\n## 下一步\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\n\\n## 资源状态\\ncqa1-38874: 无 GPU 信息\\n\\n## 告警\\n- exp008_sdgd_zero_fewnerd: 训练失败 — unknown\\n- exp012_llama_fewnerd: 训练失败 — unknown\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SXRss7eBGJPPmpwoPgRkvQ","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"f594cf49-20cc-4223-b607-e5dba70b89fe","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:45:46.828Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0166sXo7xeGCQoHKzTqWahtQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: best\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original 3-claim framing intact. LLaMA cross-model validation\n3\t  confirms pairwise confusability as generalizable signal (r≥0.10, p<0.01). SDGD v2\n4\t  with merge-dedup resolves SDGD-zero''s precision collapse, achieving F1 gains over\n5\t  single-pass on both Few-NERD and MAVEN. Centrality confound analysis elevates from\n6\t  limitation to methodological contribution. Headline: pairwise confusability is a\n7\t  generalizable, actionable signal for LLM IE schema design, operationalized via SDGD\n8\t  v2 with positive empirical results across three tasks and two models.\n9\t\n10\t  '\n11\timagined_experiments:\n12\t- name: DocRED Cross-Task Replication (96 relations)\n13\t  plan_id: exp-005\n14\t  description: 'Count curve + within-count BCS correlation on DocRED document-level\n15\t    RE (96 relation types). Scaling stress test beyond sentence-level FewRel.\n16\t\n17\t    '\n18\t  expected_results:\n19\t    count_curve:\n20\t      count_1_acc: 0.782\n21\t      count_4_acc: 0.753\n22\t      count_8_acc: 0.684\n23\t      count_16_acc: 0.591\n24\t      count_32_acc: 0.502\n25\t      count_64_acc: 0.421\n26\t      count_96_acc: 0.358\n27\t      degradation_total_pp: -42.4\n28\t    bcs_controlled:\n29\t      count_8_low_bcs_acc: 0.745\n30\t      count_8_high_bcs_acc: 0.462\n31\t      count_8_delta_pp: -28.3\n32\t      count_16_low_bcs_acc: 0.718\n33\t      count_16_high_bcs_acc: 0.431\n34\t      count_16_delta_pp: -28.7\n35\t    pairwise:\n36\t      n_pairs: 4560\n37\t      pearson_r: 0.131\n38\t      pearson_p: 3.8e-09\n39\t    vs_fewrel_80rel:\n40\t      degradation_pp: -42.4 vs -35.3\n41\t      bcs_effect_pp: -28.5 vs -31.2\n42\t      amplification_vs_16rel: ~4.1x\n43\t  result_rationale: 'Document-level RE introduces cross-sentence reasoning and coreference\n44\t    complexity, lowering baseline accuracy (~78% vs FewRel''s 86%). But the confusability\n45\t    mechanism operates at the type-assignment stage regardless of extraction granularity.\n46\t    DocRED''s 96 relation types include many fine-grained distinctions (\"place of\n47\t    birth\" vs \"place of death\", \"member of\" vs \"part of\") creating dense high-confusability\n48\t    clusters. The total degradation (42.4pp) exceeds FewRel''s 35.3pp because the\n49\t    document-level context amplifies attention dilution (Channel 1) while the denser\n50\t    relation taxonomy amplifies type confusion (Channel 2). The BCS effect (-28.5pp)\n51\t    is comparable to FewRel because confusability operates at the same mechanism;\n52\t    the slight reduction reflects the fact that some DocRED confusion is driven by\n53\t    discourse structure rather than type semantics alone. Pairwise r=0.131 replicates\n54\t    the range seen on Few-NERD (0.156) and MAVEN-MiniLM (0.120), confirming the signal\n55\t    generalizes to document-level RE.\n56\t\n57\t    '\n58\t  tables:\n59\t  - name: DocRED Count Curve\n60\t    columns:\n61\t    - count\n62\t    - accuracy\n63\t    - bcs_mean\n64\t    - n_batches\n65\t    rows:\n66\t    - - 1\n67\t      - 0.782\n68\t      - 0.0\n69\t      - 96\n70\t    - - 4\n71\t      - 0.753\n72\t      - 0.618\n73\t      - 5\n74\t    - - 8\n75\t      - 0.684\n76\t      - 0.702\n77\t      - 5\n78\t    - - 16\n79\t      - 0.591\n80\t      - 0.781\n81\t      - 5\n82\t    - - 32\n83\t      - 0.502\n84\t      - 0.874\n85\t      - 5\n86\t    - - 64\n87\t      - 0.421\n88\t      - 0.938\n89\t      - 5\n90\t    - - 96\n91\t      - 0.358\n92\t      - 0.967\n93\t      - 5\n94\t  - name: DocRED BCS-Controlled Comparison\n95\t    columns:\n96\t    - count\n97\t    - low_bcs_acc\n98\t    - high_bcs_acc\n99\t    - delta_pp\n100\t    rows:\n101\t    - - 8\n102\t      - 0.745\n103\t      - 0.462\n104\t      - -28.3\n105\t    - - 16\n106\t      - 0.718\n107\t      - 0.431\n108\t      - -28.7\n109\t  figures_description:\n110\t  - 'Count curve: monotonic accuracy decline 78.2%→35.8% across 7 count levels'\n111\t  - 'BCS-controlled bar chart: ~28pp gap at both count=8 and count=16'\n112\t  - 'Cross-dataset comparison: FewRel-16 / FewRel-80 / DocRED-96 BCS amplification\n113\t    curve'\n114\t  analysis: 'Third RE dataset confirms confusability signal on document-level extraction.\n115\t    BCS effect amplification from FewRel-16 (9.1pp) → FewRel-80 (31.2pp) → DocRED-96\n116\t    (28.5pp) shows the effect scales with schema complexity, with saturation above\n117\t    ~80 types. Pairwise r=0.131 provides 4th independent replication of pairwise confusability\n118\t    signal across tasks and schema sizes.\n119\t\n120\t    '\n121\t- name: 2×2 Interaction Effect (Count × Confusability)\n122\t  plan_id: exp-006\n123\t  description: 'Factorial design: {low_count=6, high_count=22} × {low_BCS, high_BCS}\n124\t    on Few-NERD. Tests whether count and confusability contribute additively or interact.\n125\t\n126\t    '\n127\t  expected_results:\n128\t    cell_means:\n129\t      low_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.372\n130\t      low_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.288\n131\t      high_count_low_bcs_f1: 0.318\n132\t      high_count_high_bcs_f1: 0.228\n133\t    main_effects:\n134\t      count_effect_pp: -5.7\n135\t      bcs_effect_pp: -8.7\n136\t      combined_observed_pp: -14.4\n137\t      sum_of_individual_pp: -14.4\n138\t    anova:\n139\t      count_F: 8.42\n140\t      count_p: 0.006\n141\t      bcs_F: 14.73\n142\t      bcs_p: 0.001\n143\t      interaction_F: 0.31\n144\t      interaction_p: 0.42\n145\t      model_R2: 0.74\n146\t    error_channel_decomposition:\n147\t      low_count_low_bcs_recall: 0.482\n148\t      low_count_high_bcs_recall: 0.464\n149\t      high_count_low_bcs_recall: 0.385\n150\t      high_count_high_bcs_recall: 0.362\n151\t      recall_delta_by_count: -0.099\n152\t      recall_delta_by_bcs: -0.021\n153\t      low_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.008\n154\t      low_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.042\n155\t      high_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.012\n156\t      high_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.048\n157\t      confusion_delta_by_count: 0.005\n158\t      confusion_delta_by_bcs: 0.035\n159\t  result_rationale: 'The orthogonal channel model predicts additive effects because\n160\t    count and confusability degrade extraction at different processing stages. Count→recall\n161\t    operates at the entity detection stage: more types in the prompt dilute attention,\n162\t    causing entities to be missed entirely. The recall penalty (~10pp from count=6\n163\t    to count=22) is nearly constant across BCS levels because attention dilution depends\n164\t    on prompt length, not type similarity. Confusability→type confusion operates at\n165\t    the type assignment stage: semantically similar co-present types interfere during\n166\t    label selection. The confusion rate penalty (~3.5x from low to high BCS) is nearly\n167\t    constant across count levels because type interference depends on pairwise similarity,\n168\t    not total count. The non-significant interaction (F=0.31, p=0.42) confirms these\n169\t    are independent channels. The combined penalty (14.4pp) equals the sum of individual\n170\t    penalties (5.7+8.7=14.4), ruling out multiplicative interaction. R²=0.74 indicates\n171\t    the two-factor additive model explains most of the variance in extraction quality.\n172\t\n173\t    '\n174\t  tables:\n175\t  - name: 2×2 Factorial Results (F1)\n176\t    columns:\n177\t    - ''\n178\t    - low_BCS\n179\t    - high_BCS\n180\t    - BCS_effect\n181\t    rows:\n182\t    - - count=6\n183\t      - 0.372\n184\t      - 0.288\n185\t      - -8.4pp\n186\t    - - count=22\n187\t      - 0.318\n188\t      - 0.228\n189\t      - -9.0pp\n190\t    - - count_effect\n191\t      - -5.4pp\n192\t      - -6.0pp\n193\t      - ''\n194\t  - name: ANOVA Summary\n195\t    columns:\n196\t    - source\n197\t    - F\n198\t    - p\n199\t    - eta_sq\n200\t    rows:\n201\t    - - count\n202\t      - 8.42\n203\t      - 0.006\n204\t      - 0.18\n205\t    - - BCS\n206\t      - 14.73\n207\t      - 0.001\n208\t      - 0.32\n209\t    - - count×BCS\n210\t      - 0.31\n211\t      - 0.42\n212\t      - 0.01\n213\t  figures_description:\n214\t  - 'Interaction plot: two nearly parallel lines (low-BCS, high-BCS) across count\n215\t    levels → additivity'\n216\t  - 'Error channel decomposition: recall bars decline with count (left panel), confusion\n217\t    rate bars increase with BCS (right panel)'\n218\t  analysis: 'Non-significant interaction is the key result — directly validates the\n219\t    orthogonal channel model central to Claim 2. The error decomposition shows clean\n220\t    separation: count primarily drives recall loss (9.9pp vs 2.1pp BCS effect on recall),\n221\t    while BCS primarily drives type confusion (3.5x vs 1.4x count effect on confusion\n222\t    rate). This mechanistic dissociation is the strongest evidence for the multi-channel\n223\t    degradation thesis.\n224\t\n225\t    '\n226\t- name: Systematic BCS-Binned Error Taxonomy\n227\t  plan_id: exp-007\n228\t  description: 'Systematic FP classification (intra-batch type substitution / extra-schema\n229\t    substitution / boundary error / hallucination) binned by BCS quartiles. Extends\n230\t    partial analysis from reeval_text_match to full BCS gradient.\n231\t\n232\t    '\n233\t  expected_results:\n234\t    bcs_bin_zero:\n235\t      n_fp: 6172\n236\t      intra_sub_pct: 0.0\n237\t      extra_sub_pct: 90.3\n238\t      boundary_pct: 6.8\n239\t      hallucination_pct: 2.9\n240\t    bcs_bin_low:\n241\t      n_fp: 2231\n242\t      intra_sub_pct: 1.6\n243\t      extra_sub_pct: 89.9\n244\t      boundary_pct: 5.7\n245\t      hallucination_pct: 2.8\n246\t    bcs_bin_medium:\n247\t      n_fp: 3843\n248\t      intra_sub_pct: 10.6\n249\t      extra_sub_pct: 78.1\n250\t      boundary_pct: 7.2\n251\t      hallucination_pct: 4.1\n252\t    bcs_bin_high:\n253\t      n_fp: 6188\n254\t      intra_sub_pct: 49.4\n255\t      extra_sub_pct: 30.8\n256\t      boundary_pct: 14.2\n257\t      hallucination_pct: 5.6\n258\t    correlations:\n259\t      intra_sub_vs_bcs_spearman_rho: 1.0\n260\t      intra_sub_vs_bcs_p: <0.001\n261\t      chi_square: 6952\n262\t      chi_square_p: ~0\n263\t    per_100_gold:\n264\t      bcs_zero_intra_sub: 0.0\n265\t      bcs_low_intra_sub: 3.4\n266\t      bcs_medium_intra_sub: 6.47\n267\t      bcs_high_intra_sub: 12.93\n268\t    exp2_controlled_trend: IntraSub rate 3.2% → 7.3% → 8.6% (rho=1.0, p<0.001)\n269\t  result_rationale: 'Registry already contains core results (exp007_error_taxonomy).\n270\t    Under best tier, the systematic BCS-binned analysis confirms a clean monotonic\n271\t    gradient: intra-batch type substitution dominates FP at high BCS (49.4%) while\n272\t    being nearly absent at BCS=0. The transition is smooth — BCS acts as a continuous\n273\t    predictor of error type composition, not a threshold effect. Boundary errors and\n274\t    hallucinations remain low (<15%) and BCS-independent, confirming they are not\n275\t    confusability-driven. The chi-square statistic (χ²=6952) reflects massive distributional\n276\t    shift in error types across BCS bins. This provides the mechanistic evidence for\n277\t    Channel 2: confusability literally causes the model to assign the wrong type label\n278\t    to correctly detected entities, rather than failing to detect them.\n279\t\n280\t    '\n281\t  tables:\n282\t  - name: Error Type Distribution by BCS Bin\n283\t    columns:\n284\t    - BCS_bin\n285\t    - n_FP\n286\t    - intra_sub_%\n287\t    - extra_sub_%\n288\t    - boundary_%\n289\t    - halluc_%\n290\t    rows:\n291\t    - - '0.00'\n292\t      - 6172\n293\t      - 0.0\n294\t      - 90.3\n295\t      - 6.8\n296\t      - 2.9\n297\t    - - <0.85\n298\t      - 2231\n299\t      - 1.6\n300\t      - 89.9\n301\t      - 5.7\n302\t      - 2.8\n303\t    - - 0.85-0.95\n304\t      - 3843\n305\t      - 10.6\n306\t      - 78.1\n307\t      - 7.2\n308\t      - 4.1\n309\t    - - ≥0.95\n310\t      - 6188\n311\t      - 49.4\n312\t      - 30.8\n313\t      - 14.2\n314\t      - 5.6\n315\t  figures_description:\n316\t  - 'Stacked bar chart: error type composition shifts from extra-schema dominant (BCS=0)\n317\t    to intra-batch substitution dominant (BCS≥0.95)'\n318\t  - 'Intra-sub per 100 gold entities: monotonic curve from 0 to 12.93 across BCS bins'\n319\t  analysis: 'These results (largely already in registry) are the mechanistic backbone\n320\t    of Claim 2. The near-perfect monotonic relationship (ρ=1.0) between BCS and type\n321\t    confusion proportion is the most direct evidence that semantic similarity causes\n322\t    type confusion, not merely correlates with it. The error taxonomy also rules out\n323\t    alternative explanations: hallucination and boundary errors are BCS-independent,\n324\t    confirming confusability operates specifically on the type discrimination channel.\n325\t\n326\t    '\n327\t- name: SDGD v2 on MAVEN (168 event types)\n328\t  plan_id: exp-009\n329\t  description: 'SDGD v2 (with merge-dedup) on MAVEN Event Detection. 168 event types\n330\t    partitioned into low-confusability batches via graph coloring. Compare vs single-pass,\n331\t    random batching v2, and per-type splitting v2.\n332\t\n333\t    '\n334\t  expected_results:\n335\t    single_pass:\n336\t      relaxed_f1: 0.247\n337\t      exact_f1: 0.018\n338\t      precision_relaxed: 0.268\n339\t      recall_relaxed: 0.229\n340\t      batches: 1\n341\t      time_s: 82\n342\t    sdgd_v2:\n343\t      relaxed_f1: 0.282\n344\t      exact_f1: 0.028\n345\t      precision_relaxed: 0.258\n346\t      recall_relaxed: 0.312\n347\t      batches: 12\n348\t      time_s: 485\n349\t      f1_delta_vs_single_pass_pp: '+3.5'\n350\t      precision_drop_vs_single_pass_pp: '-1.0'\n351\t    random_v2:\n352\t      relaxed_f1: 0.265\n353\t      exact_f1: 0.022\n354\t      precision_relaxed: 0.251\n355\t      recall_relaxed: 0.281\n356\t      batches: 12\n357\t      time_s: 510\n358\t    per_type_v2:\n359\t      relaxed_f1: 0.291\n360\t      exact_f1: 0.031\n361\t      precision_relaxed: 0.261\n362\t      recall_relaxed: 0.329\n363\t      batches: 168\n364\t      time_s: 6250\n365\t  result_rationale: 'MAVEN''s 168 event types create a dense confusability graph with\n366\t    many FrameNet-sibling clusters (e.g., \"Attack\" vs \"Catastrophe\" vs \"Destroying\").\n367\t    SDGD v2''s graph coloring separates these siblings across batches, reducing within-batch\n368\t    type confusion. The merge-dedup strategy is critical: SDGD-zero failed because\n369\t    multi-batch merging without dedup amplified false positives. Merge-dedup resolves\n370\t    this by (a) deduplicating overlapping trigger predictions across batches using\n371\t    span matching, and (b) selecting the highest-confidence prediction when conflicts\n372\t    arise. The +3.5pp relaxed F1 gain reflects genuine confusion reduction. The precision\n373\t    drop (-1.0pp) is within the ≤5pp budget because merge-dedup catches most spurious\n374\t    duplicates. The gain is larger than on Few-NERD (+3.5pp vs +3.5pp relaxed) in\n375\t    absolute terms because MAVEN''s 168 types provide more room for decomposition:\n376\t    more confusable pairs to separate, and the single-pass baseline is more degraded.\n377\t    Random batching v2 captures partial benefit (batch reduction + dedup) but misses\n378\t    confusability-aware separation.\n379\t\n380\t    '\n381\t  tables:\n382\t  - name: MAVEN SDGD v2 Comparison\n383\t    columns:\n384\t    - method\n385\t    - relaxed_F1\n386\t    - exact_F1\n387\t    - precision\n388\t    - recall\n389\t    - batches\n390\t    - time_s\n391\t    rows:\n392\t    - - single_pass\n393\t      - 0.247\n394\t      - 0.018\n395\t      - 0.268\n396\t      - 0.229\n397\t      - 1\n398\t      - 82\n399\t    - - sdgd_v2\n400\t      - 0.282\n401\t      - 0.028\n402\t      - 0.258\n403\t      - 0.312\n404\t      - 12\n405\t      - 485\n406\t    - - random_v2\n407\t      - 0.265\n408\t      - 0.022\n409\t      - 0.251\n410\t      - 0.281\n411\t      - 12\n412\t      - 510\n413\t    - - per_type_v2\n414\t      - 0.291\n415\t      - 0.031\n416\t      - 0.261\n417\t      - 0.329\n418\t      - 168\n419\t      - 6250\n420\t  figures_description:\n421\t  - 'Bar chart: F1 comparison across methods, SDGD v2 > single-pass > random, per-type\n422\t    as upper bound'\n423\t  - 'Precision-recall tradeoff: SDGD v2 boosts recall (+8.3pp) with minimal precision\n424\t    loss (-1.0pp)'\n425\t  analysis: 'MAVEN replication confirms SDGD v2 generalizes beyond NER. The key insight\n426\t    is that merge-dedup converts decomposition''s recall boost into net F1 gain by\n427\t    controlling precision loss. On MAVEN specifically, the improvement is driven by\n428\t    better separation of FrameNet-sibling event types that single-pass conflates.\n429\t    Cross-task (NER+ED) SDGD v2 success strengthens Claim 3.\n430\t\n431\t    '\n432\t- name: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (Empirical Calibration)\n433\t  plan_id: exp-010\n434\t  description: 'SDGD-cal v2: confusability graph from 5-10 calibration examples (empirical\n435\t    confusion matrix) instead of embedding SCS. Compare vs SDGD-zero v2 and single-pass\n436\t    on Few-NERD.\n437\t\n438\t    '\n439\t  expected_results:\n440\t    sdgd_cal_v2:\n441\t      f1: 0.493\n442\t      precision: 0.548\n443\t      recall: 0.449\n444\t      batches: 8\n445\t      time_s: 405\n446\t      calibration_calls: 660\n447\t      calibration_time_s: 320\n448\t    sdgd_zero_v2:\n449\t      f1: 0.48\n450\t      precision: 0.538\n451\t      recall: 0.433\n452\t    single_pass:\n453\t      f1: 0.445\n454\t      precision: 0.563\n455\t      recall: 0.368\n456\t    deltas:\n457\t      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp: '+1.3'\n458\t      sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp: '+4.8'\n459\t    calibration_vs_embedding:\n460\t      spearman_rho_scs_vs_empirical: 0.42\n461\t      kendall_tau_scs_vs_empirical: 0.3\n462\t      n_pair_rank_disagreements_top50: 11\n463\t  result_rationale: 'Empirical calibration measures actual model confusion on held-out\n464\t    examples, capturing model-specific biases that embedding SCS approximates but\n465\t    cannot fully predict. For example, Qwen3 may confuse \"building-library\" ↔ \"building-other\"\n466\t    more than their embedding similarity suggests (due to prompt formatting or training\n467\t    distribution), and conversely may handle \"person-politician\" ↔ \"person-scholar\"\n468\t    better than expected. These rank disagreements (11/50 in top-50 pairs) are enough\n469\t    to reshuffle the confusability graph and produce marginally better batch assignments.\n470\t    The +1.3pp gain over SDGD-zero is modest because (a) the embedding proxy is already\n471\t    ρ=0.42 correlated with empirical confusion, capturing the dominant similarity\n472\t    axis, and (b) Few-NERD''s cross-domain schema has clear cluster boundaries that\n473\t    both methods identify. The calibration overhead (660 API calls, ~5min) is acceptable\n474\t    for offline schema decomposition planning. The main value of SDGD-cal is that\n475\t    it bypasses embedding choice entirely — critical for practitioners who cannot\n476\t    determine which embedding best suits their schema a priori.\n477\t\n478\t    '\n479\t  tables:\n480\t  - name: Few-NERD SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero\n481\t    columns:\n482\t    - method\n483\t    - F1\n484\t    - precision\n485\t    - recall\n486\t    - Δ_vs_single\n487\t    rows:\n488\t    - - single_pass\n489\t      - 0.445\n490\t      - 0.563\n491\t      - 0.368\n492\t      - —\n493\t    - - sdgd_zero_v2\n494\t      - 0.48\n495\t      - 0.538\n496\t      - 0.433\n497\t      - +3.5pp\n498\t    - - sdgd_cal_v2\n499\t      - 0.493\n500\t      - 0.548\n501\t      - 0.449\n502\t      - +4.8pp\n503\t  figures_description:\n504\t  - 'Scatter: embedding SCS vs empirical confusion rate (ρ=0.42) with highlighted\n505\t    rank disagreements'\n506\t  - 'F1 ladder: single-pass < sdgd-zero < sdgd-cal with confidence intervals'\n507\t  analysis: 'SDGD-cal establishes an upper bound for embedding-free SDGD. The ρ=0.42\n508\t    correlation between SCS and empirical confusion validates the embedding proxy\n509\t    while showing room for improvement. In practice, SDGD-cal is recommended when\n510\t    calibration data is available; SDGD-zero when it is not. Both outperform single-pass,\n511\t    confirming Claim 3.\n512\t\n513\t    '\n514\t- name: SDGD-cal on MAVEN (Empirical Calibration, 168 types)\n515\t  plan_id: exp-011\n516\t  description: 'SDGD-cal v2 on MAVEN with empirical confusion matrix from calibration\n517\t    examples. Full comparison table: single-pass / SDGD-zero v2 / SDGD-cal v2 / random\n518\t    v2 / per-type v2.\n519\t\n520\t    '\n521\t  expected_results:\n522\t    sdgd_cal_v2:\n523\t      relaxed_f1: 0.298\n524\t      exact_f1: 0.032\n525\t      precision_relaxed: 0.264\n526\t      recall_relaxed: 0.342\n527\t      batches: 12\n528\t      calibration_calls: 1680\n529\t      calibration_time_s: 810\n530\t    sdgd_zero_v2:\n531\t      relaxed_f1: 0.282\n532\t      exact_f1: 0.028\n533\t    single_pass:\n534\t      relaxed_f1: 0.247\n535\t      exact_f1: 0.018\n536\t    deltas:\n537\t      sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp: '+1.6'\n538\t      sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp: '+5.1'\n539\t  result_rationale: 'SDGD-cal''s advantage over SDGD-zero is slightly larger on MAVEN\n540\t    (+1.6pp vs +1.3pp on Few-NERD) because MAVEN''s homogeneous FrameNet-based schema\n541\t    makes embedding similarity less discriminative. Many event type pairs have uniformly\n542\t    high SCS (mean=0.216, narrow distribution), so the embedding proxy struggles to\n543\t    rank confusability among mid-range pairs. Empirical calibration captures within-cluster\n544\t    confusion structure that embedding cosine similarity compresses: \"Attack\" and\n545\t    \"Catastrophe\" may have similar SCS to \"Attack\" and \"Protest\", but the actual confusion\n546\t    rates differ substantially due to trigger word overlap patterns. The 1680 calibration\n547\t    calls (168 types × 10 examples) are the main cost, but acceptable for a one-time\n548\t    schema analysis. The +5.1pp gain over single-pass on MAVEN is the largest SDGD\n549\t    improvement across all experiments, reflecting MAVEN''s 168-type schema as the\n550\t    highest-complexity setting.\n551\t\n552\t    '\n553\t  tables:\n554\t  - name: MAVEN Full Comparison Table\n555\t    columns:\n556\t    - method\n557\t    - relaxed_F1\n558\t    - exact_F1\n559\t    - batches\n560\t    - total_time_s\n561\t    rows:\n562\t    - - single_pass\n563\t      - 0.247\n564\t      - 0.018\n565\t      - 1\n566\t      - 82\n567\t    - - random_v2\n568\t      - 0.265\n569\t      - 0.022\n570\t      - 12\n571\t      - 510\n572\t    - - sdgd_zero_v2\n573\t      - 0.282\n574\t      - 0.028\n575\t      - 12\n576\t      - 485\n577\t    - - sdgd_cal_v2\n578\t      - 0.298\n579\t      - 0.032\n580\t      - 12\n581\t      - 1295\n582\t    - - per_type_v2\n583\t      - 0.291\n584\t      - 0.031\n585\t      - 168\n586\t      - 6250\n587\t  figures_description:\n588\t  - 'Full comparison bar chart: SDGD-cal v2 achieves highest F1, surpassing even per-type\n589\t    v2'\n590\t  - 'Calibration ROI plot: F1 gain per additional API call for each method'\n591\t  analysis: 'Notably, SDGD-cal v2 (0.298) slightly exceeds per-type v2 (0.291) on\n592\t    MAVEN. This is because per-type splitting eliminates within-batch confusion but\n593\t    loses ALL cross-type context, while SDGD-cal retains beneficial cross-type context\n594\t    (non-confusable types in the same batch help the model discriminate). This inverted\n595\t    result — SDGD-cal > per-type — is a strong argument for confusability-AWARE decomposition\n596\t    over naive per-type splitting. It also addresses the UniNER comparison directly.\n597\t\n598\t    '\n599\t- name: LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\n600\t  plan_id: exp-012\n601\t  description: 'Full replication of exp-002 protocol with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct on\n602\t    Few-NERD. Count curve + BCS-controlled batches + pairwise analysis + per-type\n603\t    analysis.\n604\t\n605\t    '\n606\t  expected_results:\n607\t    count_curve:\n608\t      count_1:\n609\t        f1: 0.351\n610\t        precision: 0.248\n611\t        recall: 0.598\n612\t      count_6:\n613\t        f1: 0.328\n614\t        precision: 0.258\n615\t        recall: 0.451\n616\t      count_12:\n617\t        f1: 0.364\n618\t        precision: 0.396\n619\t        recall: 0.337\n620\t      count_22:\n621\t        f1: 0.318\n622\t        precision: 0.372\n623\t        recall: 0.278\n624\t      count_44:\n625\t        f1: 0.432\n626\t        precision: 0.51\n627\t        recall: 0.375\n628\t      count_64:\n629\t        f1: 0.408\n630\t        precision: 0.625\n631\t        recall: 0.302\n632\t    pairwise_analysis:\n633\t      all_pairs:\n634\t        n_pairs: 1953\n635\t        pearson_r: 0.138\n636\t        pearson_p: 2.1e-09\n637\t        spearman_rho: 0.142\n638\t        spearman_p: 8.4e-10\n639\t      nonzero_only:\n640\t        spearman_rho: 0.221\n641\t        spearman_p: 3.5e-05\n642\t      count_12:\n643\t        pearson_r: 0.129\n644\t        pearson_p: 1.8e-08\n645\t    extreme_batch_confusion:\n646\t      count_6:\n647\t        high_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.058\n648\t        low_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.003\n649\t        ratio: 19.3\n650\t      count_12:\n651\t        high_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.041\n652\t        low_bcs_confusion_rate: 0.005\n653\t        ratio: 8.2\n654\t    bcs_controlled:\n655\t      count_6_low_bcs_f1: 0.358\n656\t      count_6_high_bcs_f1: 0.271\n657\t      count_6_delta_pp: -8.7\n658\t      count_12_low_bcs_f1: 0.391\n659\t      count_12_high_bcs_f1: 0.286\n660\t      count_12_delta_pp: -10.5\n661\t    per_type_analysis:\n662\t      n_types: 63\n663\t      spearman_rho: -0.248\n664\t      spearman_p: 0.049\n665\t      partial_r_ctrl_logfreq: -0.172\n666\t      partial_p: 0.178\n667\t    cross_model_comparison:\n668\t      qwen3_pairwise_r: 0.156\n669\t      llama_pairwise_r: 0.138\n670\t      qwen3_confusion_ratio_range: 10-28x\n671\t      llama_confusion_ratio_range: 8-19x\n672\t      qwen3_bcs_delta_count12: -10.1\n673\t      llama_bcs_delta_count12: -10.5\n674\t  result_rationale: 'LLaMA-3.3-70B exhibits the same pairwise confusability signal\n675\t    as Qwen3-32B because the underlying mechanism — semantically similar co-present\n676\t    types cause type interference during label assignment — is architecture-independent.\n677\t    Both models are decoder-only transformers that process type labels as natural\n678\t    language tokens; when two labels share semantic content, the model''s next-token\n679\t    prediction distributes probability mass across both, increasing confusion. The\n680\t    effect size is slightly attenuated (r=0.138 vs 0.156) because LLaMA-70B''s larger\n681\t    parameter count provides marginally better type discrimination capacity. The confusion\n682\t    ratio (8-19x) is in the same order as Qwen3 (10-28x) but lower because the 70B\n683\t    model makes fewer type confusion errors per entity overall. The per-type signal\n684\t    is weaker (ρ=-0.248, borderline p=0.049) because per-type aggregation introduces\n685\t    noise and LLaMA''s different tokenization creates slight rank perturbations. Critically,\n686\t    the pairwise correlation (r=0.138, p=2.1e-9) is highly significant and falls within\n687\t    the best-tier range [0.10, 0.20], confirming the signal is not model-specific.\n688\t    The BCS-controlled delta (-10.5pp at count_12) falls within the cross-model range\n689\t    [-35, -15]pp when scaled to larger schemas.\n690\t\n691\t    '\n692\t  tables:\n693\t  - name: Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\n694\t    columns:\n695\t    - metric\n696\t    - Qwen3-32B\n697\t    - LLaMA-70B\n698\t    rows:\n699\t    - - pairwise Pearson r\n700\t      - 0.156\n701\t      - 0.138\n702\t    - - pairwise p-value\n703\t      - '4.6e-12'\n704\t      - '2.1e-09'\n705\t    - - nonzero Spearman ρ\n706\t      - 0.254\n707\t      - 0.221\n708\t    - - confusion ratio (count_6 extreme)\n709\t      - 27.5x\n710\t      - 19.3x\n711\t    - - confusion ratio (count_12 extreme)\n712\t      - 9.9x\n713\t      - 8.2x\n714\t    - - BCS-controlled Δ (count_12)\n715\t      - -10.1pp\n716\t      - -10.5pp\n717\t    - - per-type ρ\n718\t      - -0.293\n719\t      - -0.248\n720\t  - name: LLaMA Count Curve\n721\t    columns:\n722\t    - count\n723\t    - F1\n724\t    - precision\n725\t    - recall\n726\t    rows:\n727\t    - - 1\n728\t      - 0.351\n729\t      - 0.248\n730\t      - 0.598\n731\t    - - 6\n732\t      - 0.328\n733\t      - 0.258\n734\t      - 0.451\n735\t    - - 12\n736\t      - 0.364\n737\t      - 0.396\n738\t      - 0.337\n739\t    - - 22\n740\t      - 0.318\n741\t      - 0.372\n742\t      - 0.278\n743\t    - - 44\n744\t      - 0.432\n745\t      - 0.51\n746\t      - 0.375\n747\t    - - 64\n748\t      - 0.408\n749\t      - 0.625\n750\t      - 0.302\n751\t  figures_description:\n752\t  - 'Side-by-side pairwise scatter: SCS vs confusion count for Qwen3 (left) and LLaMA\n753\t    (right), both showing positive slope'\n754\t  - 'Cross-model count curve overlay: similar recall decline pattern, LLaMA slightly\n755\t    lower baseline'\n756\t  - 'Confusion ratio comparison: bar chart showing consistent high/low BCS gap across\n757\t    both models'\n758\t  analysis: 'Cross-model validation is the single most important experiment for reviewer\n759\t    concern mitigation. Both models agree on direction, significance, and approximate\n760\t    magnitude of the pairwise confusability effect. The residual differences (r=0.156\n761\t    vs 0.138, ratio 28x vs 19x) are consistent with model capacity differences rather\n762\t    than contradictory findings. This eliminates the \"model-specific artifact\" concern\n763\t    and allows the paper to claim the confusability signal is a property of the task\n764\t    structure, not the model.\n765\t\n766\t    '\n767\t- name: Random Batching Ablation (Same k as SDGD)\n768\t  plan_id: exp-013\n769\t  description: 'SDGD v2 vs random batching v2 (same batch count k=8) on Few-NERD.\n770\t    Both use merge-dedup. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\n771\t\n772\t    '\n773\t  expected_results:\n774\t    sdgd_v2:\n775\t      f1: 0.48\n776\t      precision: 0.538\n777\t      recall: 0.433\n778\t      batches: 8\n779\t      time_s: 395\n780\t      std_across_5_seeds: 0.008\n781\t    random_v2:\n782\t      f1: 0.458\n783\t      precision: 0.521\n784\t      recall: 0.411\n785\t      batches: 8\n786\t      time_s: 410\n787\t      std_across_5_seeds: 0.015\n788\t    single_pass:\n789\t      f1: 0.445\n790\t      precision: 0.563\n791\t      recall: 0.368\n792\t    deltas:\n793\t      sdgd_v2_vs_random_v2_pp: '+2.2'\n794\t      random_v2_vs_single_pass_pp: '+1.3'\n795\t      sdgd_v2_vs_single_pass_pp: '+3.5'\n796\t    decomposition_attribution:\n797\t      batch_reduction_contribution_pp: 1.3\n798\t      confusability_aware_grouping_pp: 2.2\n799\t      total_sdgd_gain_pp: 3.5\n800\t      pct_from_grouping: 62.9\n801\t  result_rationale: 'Merge-dedup resolves the core precision problem for ALL decomposition\n802\t    strategies, so both random and SDGD benefit equally from dedup. The remaining\n803\t    difference (2.2pp) isolates the value of confusability-aware batch composition.\n804\t    Random batching gets 1.3pp from batch reduction alone (smaller batches → better\n805\t    per-entity attention → higher recall, with dedup controlling precision). SDGD\n806\t    adds 2.2pp by ensuring confusable type pairs are separated, reducing within-batch\n807\t    type confusion. The 62.9% attribution to grouping (vs 37.1% to batch reduction)\n808\t    quantifies SDGD''s specific contribution. SDGD also shows lower variance (σ=0.008\n809\t    vs 0.015) because its batch composition is deterministic and optimized, while\n810\t    random batching quality fluctuates depending on which confusable types happen\n811\t    to co-occur. The lower variance is practically important — SDGD gives predictable\n812\t    quality.\n813\t\n814\t    '\n815\t  tables:\n816\t  - name: 'Ablation: Grouping vs Batch Reduction'\n817\t    columns:\n818\t    - method\n819\t    - F1\n820\t    - Δ_vs_single\n821\t    - σ(5 seeds)\n822\t    - source_of_gain\n823\t    rows:\n824\t    - - single_pass\n825\t      - 0.445\n826\t      - —\n827\t      - —\n828\t      - —\n829\t    - - random_v2 (k=8)\n830\t      - 0.458\n831\t      - +1.3pp\n832\t      - 0.015\n833\t      - batch reduction only\n834\t    - - sdgd_v2 (k=8)\n835\t      - 0.48\n836\t      - +3.5pp\n837\t      - 0.008\n838\t      - batch reduction + grouping\n839\t  figures_description:\n840\t  - 'Stacked attribution bar: total SDGD gain decomposed into batch-reduction (37%)\n841\t    and confusability-grouping (63%)'\n842\t  - 'Box plot: 5-seed F1 distributions for random v2 vs SDGD v2, showing SDGD''s tighter\n843\t    spread'\n844\t  analysis: 'This ablation is essential for Claim 3: it proves SDGD''s value is primarily\n845\t    from confusability-aware grouping (63%), not mere batch reduction. Without this\n846\t    ablation, a reviewer could argue that any decomposition strategy would work equally\n847\t    well. The 2.2pp gap, combined with SDGD''s lower variance, makes a clean case\n848\t    for principled decomposition.\n849\t\n850\t    '\n851\t- name: Per-Type Splitting Ablation (UniNER-style)\n852\t  plan_id: exp-014\n853\t  description: 'Per-type splitting v2 (1 type per batch, k=66) on Few-NERD with merge-dedup.\n854\t    Upper bound on decomposition benefit. Quality-latency tradeoff vs SDGD v2.\n855\t\n856\t    '\n857\t  expected_results:\n858\t    per_type_v2:\n859\t      f1: 0.492\n860\t      precision: 0.545\n861\t      recall: 0.449\n862\t      batches: 66\n863\t      time_s: 2480\n864\t      api_calls: 66\n865\t    sdgd_v2:\n866\t      f1: 0.48\n867\t      precision: 0.538\n868\t      recall: 0.433\n869\t      batches: 8\n870\t      time_s: 395\n871\t      api_calls: 8\n872\t    single_pass:\n873\t      f1: 0.445\n874\t      precision: 0.563\n875\t      recall: 0.368\n876\t      batches: 1\n877\t      time_s: 78\n878\t      api_calls: 1\n879\t    quality_vs_latency:\n880\t      sdgd_v2_quality_pct_of_per_type: 97.6\n881\t      sdgd_v2_latency_pct_of_per_type: 15.9\n882\t      sdgd_v2_api_call_pct_of_per_type: 12.1\n883\t    per_type_vs_sdgd_delta_pp: '+1.2'\n884\t    residual_confusion_analysis:\n885\t      sdgd_residual_confusion_pairs: 23\n886\t      mean_scs_of_residual: 0.612\n887\t      max_scs_of_residual: 0.743\n888\t  result_rationale: 'Per-type splitting eliminates within-batch type confusion entirely\n889\t    — with one type per batch, there is no co-present type to confuse with. Merge-dedup\n890\t    handles cross-batch deduplication. The resulting F1 (0.492) is the theoretical\n891\t    ceiling for decomposition-based approaches. SDGD v2 achieves 97.6% of this ceiling\n892\t    at 15.9% of the latency because graph coloring concentrates separation effort\n893\t    on the most confusable pairs (SCS > 0.8), while allowing low-similarity types\n894\t    to share batches safely. The residual 1.2pp gap comes from 23 moderately confusable\n895\t    pairs (mean SCS=0.612) that SDGD groups together to keep k=8. These pairs cause\n896\t    occasional type confusion that per-type avoids entirely. The tradeoff is clear:\n897\t    1.2pp F1 costs 6.3x latency and 8.25x API calls. For production deployment, SDGD\n898\t    v2 dominates per-type splitting on cost-effectiveness. For offline analysis where\n899\t    latency is irrelevant, per-type v2 provides the best quality.\n900\t\n901\t    '\n902\t  tables:\n903\t  - name: Quality-Latency Pareto Frontier\n904\t    columns:\n905\t    - method\n906\t    - F1\n907\t    - batches\n908\t    - time_s\n909\t    - F1_per_minute\n910\t    rows:\n911\t    - - single_pass\n912\t      - 0.445\n913\t      - 1\n914\t      - 78\n915\t      - 0.342\n916\t    - - sdgd_v2\n917\t      - 0.48\n918\t      - 8\n919\t      - 395\n920\t      - 0.073\n921\t    - - per_type_v2\n922\t      - 0.492\n923\t      - 66\n924\t      - 2480\n925\t      - 0.012\n926\t  figures_description:\n927\t  - 'Pareto curve: F1 vs latency, showing SDGD v2 at the Pareto-optimal knee'\n928\t  - 'Residual confusion heatmap: 23 type pairs contributing to SDGD''s 1.2pp gap vs\n929\t    per-type'\n930\t  analysis: 'The per-type ablation addresses the UniNER comparison directly. UniNER-style\n931\t    per-type splitting provides the highest quality but at prohibitive cost for real-time\n932\t    applications. SDGD v2 offers a principled middle ground — it captures 97.6% of\n933\t    the quality with a data-driven criterion for WHICH types need separation. This\n934\t    is the operational contribution: not just \"decompose the schema\" but \"decompose\n935\t    it optimally given a latency budget.\"\n936\t\n937\t    '\n938\t- name: SCS Proxy Validation (Embedding vs Empirical Confusion)\n939\t  plan_id: exp-015\n940\t  description: 'Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix.\n941\t    Validates embedding proxy for SDGD-zero practical use.\n942\t\n943\t    '\n944\t  expected_results:\n945\t    fewnerd_qwen3:\n946\t      spearman_rho: 0.43\n947\t      kendall_tau: 0.31\n948\t      n_pairs_with_confusion: 287\n949\t      top10_overlap: 7\n950\t    maven_minilm:\n951\t      spearman_rho: 0.38\n952\t      kendall_tau: 0.27\n953\t      n_pairs_with_confusion: 1842\n954\t      top10_overlap: 6\n955\t    maven_qwen3:\n956\t      spearman_rho: 0.15\n957\t      kendall_tau: 0.11\n958\t      n_pairs_with_confusion: 1842\n959\t      top10_overlap: 2\n960\t    fewnerd_minilm:\n961\t      spearman_rho: 0.22\n962\t      kendall_tau: 0.16\n963\t      n_pairs_with_confusion: 287\n964\t      top10_overlap: 4\n965\t    cross_validation:\n966\t      best_embedding_per_dataset:\n967\t        fewnerd: Qwen3 (ρ=0.43)\n968\t        maven: MiniLM (ρ=0.38)\n969\t      pattern: extraction-model embeddings excel on cross-domain schemas; generic\n970\t        embeddings excel on homogeneous schemas\n971\t  result_rationale: 'The SCS-confusion correlation (ρ=0.38-0.43 for best embedding\n972\t    per dataset) validates the embedding proxy as a reasonable but imperfect predictor\n973\t    of actual model confusion. The imperfection is expected: embedding SCS captures\n974\t    semantic similarity (the dominant confusion axis) but not model-specific factors\n975\t    like tokenization artifacts, prompt position effects, or label frequency biases.\n976\t    The 2×2 pattern (Qwen3 better for Few-NERD, MiniLM better for MAVEN) replicates\n977\t    the D010 finding at the validated pairwise level. For Few-NERD''s cross-domain\n978\t    schema, Qwen3 embeddings align with the extraction model''s internal representations,\n979\t    capturing model-specific confusability. For MAVEN''s homogeneous FrameNet schema,\n980\t    Qwen3 compresses within-domain distinctions while MiniLM''s generic sentence embedding\n981\t    preserves them. The top-10 overlap (6-7/10) shows that both best-embedding conditions\n982\t    identify the most confusable pairs with reasonable fidelity, validating SDGD-zero''s\n983\t    practical utility. The gap between ρ=0.43 and a hypothetical perfect proxy (ρ=1.0)\n984\t    explains SDGD-cal''s incremental gain.\n985\t\n986\t    '\n987\t  tables:\n988\t  - name: SCS Proxy Validation Matrix\n989\t    columns:\n990\t    - dataset\n991\t    - embedding\n992\t    - Spearman_ρ\n993\t    - Kendall_τ\n994\t    - top10_overlap\n995\t    rows:\n996\t    - - Few-NERD\n997\t      - Qwen3\n998\t      - 0.43\n999\t      - 0.31\n1000\t      - 7/10\n1001\t    - - Few-NERD\n1002\t      - MiniLM\n1003\t      - 0.22\n1004\t      - 0.16\n1005\t      - 4/10\n1006\t    - - MAVEN\n1007\t      - MiniLM\n1008\t      - 0.38\n1009\t      - 0.27\n1010\t      - 6/10\n1011\t    - - MAVEN\n1012\t      - Qwen3\n1013\t      - 0.15\n1014\t      - 0.11\n1015\t      - 2/10\n1016\t  figures_description:\n1017\t  - '2×2 heatmap: Spearman ρ for each (dataset, embedding) cell, highlighting diagonal\n1018\t    as best'\n1019\t  - 'Scatter: SCS vs confusion rate for best embedding per dataset, with regression\n1020\t    line and ρ annotation'\n1021\t  analysis: 'Validates SDGD-zero as a practical no-calibration method while explaining\n1022\t    why SDGD-cal improves: the ρ=0.43 ceiling for embedding-based proxy means ~18%\n1023\t    of variance in confusion is captured, leaving room for empirical calibration.\n1024\t    The cross-embedding pattern provides practical guidance: use extraction-model\n1025\t    embeddings for diverse schemas, generic embeddings for domain-specific schemas,\n1026\t    or SDGD-cal to bypass the choice entirely. This completes the SDGD framework:\n1027\t    SDGD-zero for zero-cost deployment, SDGD-cal for maximum quality.'\n1028\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"335448d1-4993-4474-887b-62088775698f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:45:47.357Z"}
