{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016JCDt7PQeGU7u7jvAvEbr4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y8poSokqMVuxW6fKsr4TkU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/median/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16177,"cache_read_input_tokens":74311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16177,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"51db426d-3fe1-44fe-819a-958ac01c1690"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y8poSokqMVuxW6fKsr4TkU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Multi-Channel Schema Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction:\\\\Pairwise Confusability, Merge Amplification, and the Limits of Decomposition}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\date{}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t%% ============================================================\n23\t%% ABSTRACT\n24\t%% ============================================================\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tTraining-free LLM information extraction (IE) degrades severely on complex schemas, yet the mechanisms behind this degradation remain poorly understood. We present the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count across three IE tasks: named entity recognition (Few-NERD, 66 types), document-level relation extraction (DocRED, 96 types), and event detection (MAVEN, 168 types). We identify a \\emph{multi-channel degradation} model: type count primarily causes entity omission (recall decline), while pairwise type confusability causes type confusion errors---and these channels operate additively ($p=0.14$ for interaction). Pairwise confusability, measured via embedding-based Schema Confusability Scores (SCS), is a robust cross-task signal ($\\rho=0.35$, $p<10^{-4}$), validated across models (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B). We operationalize this signal into Schema DAG Decomposition (SDGD), a confusability-minimizing batching framework. SDGD consistently fails to outperform single-pass extraction, and our analysis reveals \\emph{merge amplification}---the compounding of deduplication errors when reassembling batch outputs---as the fundamental bottleneck. Even with oracle merge mitigation (SDGD~v2), decomposition achieves only $-2.5$ F1 points versus single-pass at $5\\times$ latency cost. These findings explain why prior decomposition methods (UniNER, LLM-PEE) report only modest gains and provide actionable guidance for future schema-aware IE systems.\n27\t\\end{abstract}\n28\t\n29\t%% ============================================================\n30\t%% 1  INTRODUCTION\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t\n34\tLarge language models achieve competitive information extraction performance through in-context learning, eliminating the need for task-specific training data. However, a persistent gap remains on complex schemas: when the number of target types grows beyond 20--30, training-free LLM IE suffers 20--60 F1 point degradation compared to supervised baselines. This \\emph{schema complexity} bottleneck is the primary obstacle to deploying LLM-based IE in real-world settings such as clinical NLP, regulatory compliance, and low-resource multilingual applications where labeled data is unavailable.\n35\t\n36\tExisting decomposition approaches---splitting a large schema into smaller sub-tasks---operate on unvalidated assumptions about what makes schemas complex for LLMs. UniNER~\\citep{universal-ner} splits into per-type queries, gaining 3.3 F1 but losing cross-type context. LLM-PEE uses embedding similarity as an implicit confusability proxy without empirical validation. No prior work isolates the independent contributions of \\emph{type count} (how many types) and \\emph{type confusability} (how similar the types are) to extraction degradation, nor characterizes the failure modes of decomposition-based approaches.\n37\t\n38\tWe address these gaps through a controlled empirical study spanning three IE tasks (NER, document-level RE, event detection), two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and two embedding models (Qwen3, MiniLM). Our contributions are:\n39\t\n40\t\\begin{enumerate}\n41\t    \\item \\textbf{Multi-channel degradation model.} We demonstrate that count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels---count causes recall decline via attention dilution, confusability causes type confusion via semantic interference---and these channels combine additively (Section~\\ref{sec:multichannel}).\n42\t\n43\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise confusability as a robust signal.} We validate that embedding-based pairwise Schema Confusability Scores (SCS) predict empirical type confusion ($\\rho=0.35$, $p<10^{-4}$) across tasks and models, while per-type aggregation introduces centrality confounds in homogeneous schemas (Section~\\ref{sec:confusability}).\n44\t\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Merge amplification as a fundamental bottleneck.} Through systematic decomposition experiments (SDGD-zero, SDGD-cal, random batching, per-type splitting, SDGD~v2), we identify merge amplification---the compounding of deduplication errors during output reassembly---as the binding constraint that prevents decomposition from outperforming single-pass extraction (Section~\\ref{sec:decomposition}).\n46\t\\end{enumerate}\n47\t\n48\t%% ============================================================\n49\t%% 2  RELATED WORK\n50\t%% ============================================================\n51\t\\section{Related Work}\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.}\n54\tIn-context learning approaches for NER, RE, and event detection prompt LLMs with task descriptions and type definitions. Performance scales with model size and prompt engineering but degrades with schema complexity. Recent work explores structured prompting and chain-of-thought reasoning for IE, but the schema complexity bottleneck persists.\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{Schema decomposition for IE.}\n57\tUniNER~\\citep{universal-ner} decomposes NER into per-type queries, achieving gains on diverse schemas but at high latency cost. LLM-PEE groups similar types into batches using embedding similarity but does not validate whether similarity predicts confusion. GoLLIE and InstructUIE use schema-aware prompting without explicit decomposition. None of these methods characterize \\emph{when} and \\emph{why} decomposition helps or hurts.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Schema complexity analysis.}\n60\tPrior work studies label confusion in supervised IE through confusion matrices and error analysis, but controlled studies of schema complexity effects on LLM IE are absent. The closest parallel is ``Lost in the Middle''~\\citep{lost-in-middle}, which conducts controlled position-bias experiments on LLM retrieval---we adopt a similar methodology for schema-bias experiments on LLM IE.\n61\t\n62\t%% ============================================================\n63\t%% 3  STUDY DESIGN\n64\t%% ============================================================\n65\t\\section{Study Design}\n66\t\\label{sec:design}\n67\t\n68\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation Framework}\n69\t\n70\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation operates through two independent channels:\n71\t\n72\t\\begin{itemize}\n73\t    \\item \\textbf{Channel 1 (Count $\\to$ Recall):} More types in the prompt cause attention dilution, leading to entity omission. Degradation is monotonic and addressable by reducing batch size.\n74\t    \\item \\textbf{Channel 2 (Confusability $\\to$ Type Confusion):} Semantically similar co-present types cause interference during type assignment, leading to correct spans labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types.\n75\t\\end{itemize}\n76\t\n77\tIf these channels are orthogonal, their effects should be additive in a factorial design, with no significant interaction term.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Schema Confusability Score (SCS)}\n80\t\n81\tWe define the \\emph{pairwise Schema Confusability Score} between types $t_i$ and $t_j$ as the cosine similarity of their label embeddings:\n82\t\\begin{equation}\n83\t    \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \\frac{\\mathbf{e}(t_i) \\cdot \\mathbf{e}(t_j)}{\\|\\mathbf{e}(t_i)\\| \\|\\mathbf{e}(t_j)\\|}\n84\t\\end{equation}\n85\twhere $\\mathbf{e}(\\cdot)$ is an embedding model (Qwen3-32B or MiniLM-L6-v2). For batch-level analysis, we define the \\emph{Batch Confusability Score} (BCS) as the mean pairwise SCS within a batch.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{SDGD: Schema DAG Decomposition}\n88\t\\label{sec:sdgd-design}\n89\t\n90\tSDGD partitions a schema's types into $k$ batches by constructing a pairwise confusability graph $G = (V, E)$ where vertices are types and edge weights are pairwise SCS values. We threshold edges at the median SCS and apply graph coloring to minimize intra-batch confusability. We evaluate three variants:\n91\t\n92\t\\begin{itemize}\n93\t    \\item \\textbf{SDGD-zero}: Uses embedding SCS directly (no calibration data).\n94\t    \\item \\textbf{SDGD-cal}: Replaces embedding SCS with empirical confusion estimates from 8--10 calibration examples per type.\n95\t    \\item \\textbf{SDGD~v2}: Adds merge-phase mitigation via confidence thresholding and duplicate suppression.\n96\t\\end{itemize}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Datasets and Models}\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"662ae7a8-8e90-4839-a734-f1f044cb2a4b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:46:00.124Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8a62bf645e0d738f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/worst/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":90964,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":46459},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"d5fcc21d-044f-443f-89a9-abd96e9ee168","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78"}
