{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QJUnBasX973dK4WYqT1MPx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{array}\n12\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n13\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition\\\\for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tTraining-free LLM information extraction degrades sharply as schema complexity grows, yet the mechanisms underlying this degradation remain poorly understood. We present a controlled empirical study isolating two hypothesized degradation channels---type count and type confusability---across NER, RE, and ED tasks, two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and three embedding spaces. Our results yield three negative findings that challenge prevailing assumptions: (1)~type confusability, measured via embedding similarity, predicts extraction errors for Qwen3 ($r{=}0.156$, $p{<}10^{-11}$) but produces null results on LLaMA ($r{=}0.018$, $p{=}0.42$), revealing it as a model-specific sensitivity rather than a universal schema property; (2)~schema decomposition via confusability-aware graph coloring (SDGD) consistently \\emph{underperforms} both single-pass extraction and random batching ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.061$ F1 vs.\\ random, $p{=}0.003$), even when empirical confusion matrices replace embedding proxies; (3)~improved merge strategies reduce conflict rates but cannot overcome the structural precision loss inherent to decompose-and-merge ($-14.7$ F1 vs.\\ single-pass). These results establish merge amplification and proxy inadequacy as dual bottlenecks that doom one-size-fits-all decomposition, and reframe schema complexity as a model-schema interaction rather than an intrinsic schema property.\n27\t\\end{abstract}\n28\t\n29\t%--------------------------------------------------\n30\t\\section{Introduction}\n31\t\\label{sec:intro}\n32\t%--------------------------------------------------\n33\t\n34\tTraining-free LLM information extraction---where a pre-trained language model extracts structured information from text given only a schema description in the prompt---has emerged as an attractive paradigm for domains where labeled data is scarce or schemas change frequently \\cite{wang2023gptner,li2023evaluating}. However, performance degrades severely as schema complexity grows: a 20--60 F1 gap separates training-free from supervised methods on schemas with tens of types \\cite{lu2023chameleon}.\n35\t\n36\tA natural hypothesis is that this degradation arises from two orthogonal channels: (1)~\\textbf{type count}, which dilutes the model's attention across more candidate types, causing entity omission; and (2)~\\textbf{type confusability}, where semantically similar co-present types interfere during type assignment, causing type confusion errors. If these channels are indeed orthogonal and independently addressable, then principled schema decomposition---partitioning types into low-confusability batches---should recover much of the lost performance.\n37\t\n38\tWe set out to test this hypothesis through a comprehensive controlled study spanning three IE tasks (NER on Few-NERD, ED on MAVEN, RE on DocRED), two LLMs (Qwen3-32B-Instruct, LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct), and three embedding spaces (Qwen3, LLaMA, MiniLM). We developed SDGD (Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition), a framework that operationalizes pairwise type confusability into graph coloring-based schema decomposition, with variants using embedding similarity (SDGD-zero), empirical calibration (SDGD-cal), and improved merge strategies (SDGD-v2).\n39\t\n40\tOur findings are predominantly negative, but we argue they constitute important contributions:\n41\t\n42\t\\begin{itemize}\n43\t\\item \\textbf{Confusability is model-specific, not universal.} Embedding-based confusability predicts Qwen3's errors ($r{=}0.156$) but not LLaMA's ($r{=}0.018$). The two models exhibit fundamentally different error profiles, making one-size-fits-all decomposition untenable.\n44\t\n45\t\\item \\textbf{Decompose-and-merge faces dual bottlenecks.} SDGD fails not because of a single fixable issue, but because (a)~confusability proxies are too weak ($\\rho{=}0.05$--$0.18$ vs.\\ empirical confusion) and (b)~merge amplification---the structural precision loss from recombining independently extracted results---is irreducible.\n46\t\n47\t\\item \\textbf{Count and confusability interact sub-additively.} A $2{\\times}2$ factorial experiment shows the channels share a degradation floor ($F{=}0.42$, $p{=}0.52$ for interaction), undermining claims of clean orthogonality.\n48\t\\end{itemize}\n49\t\n50\tWe reframe these negative results as methodological contributions: multi-channel degradation analysis as a diagnostic tool, embedding ablation as a necessary step before decomposition, and merge amplification as a fundamental constraint on the decompose-and-merge paradigm.\n51\t\n52\t%--------------------------------------------------\n53\t\\section{Related Work}\n54\t\\label{sec:related}\n55\t%--------------------------------------------------\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.} GPT-NER \\cite{wang2023gptner} and similar approaches prompt LLMs with type definitions for zero-shot extraction. Performance drops sharply with schema size \\cite{lu2023chameleon,li2023evaluating}. UniNER \\cite{zhou2024universalner} addresses this by per-type splitting, gaining 3.3~F1 but losing cross-type context. LLM-PEE uses type similarity as an implicit decomposition criterion but never validates it empirically.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Schema decomposition.} Prior decomposition methods operate on unvalidated assumptions about what makes schemas complex. No work has isolated confusability from count, characterized when aggregated confusability measures lose signal, or tested decomposition across multiple LLMs.\n60\t\n61\t\\paragraph{Negative results in NLP.} The value of negative results has been increasingly recognized \\cite{rogers2020primer}. ``Lost in the Middle'' \\cite{liu2024lost} demonstrated systematic position biases through controlled experiments. We adopt a similar methodology to characterize schema complexity.\n62\t\n63\t%--------------------------------------------------\n64\t\\section{Framework}\n65\t\\label{sec:framework}\n66\t%--------------------------------------------------\n67\t\n68\t\\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation Model}\n69\t\n70\tWe hypothesize that schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM IE operates through two channels:\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Channel 1: Count $\\rightarrow$ Recall.} As the number of types $|\\mathcal{T}|$ in the prompt increases, the model's attention is diluted, causing entity omission. We measure this via recall degradation across controlled type counts.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Channel 2: Confusability $\\rightarrow$ Type Confusion.} When semantically similar types co-occur in a prompt, the model confuses type assignments. We quantify confusability using the Schema Confusability Score (SCS)---the cosine similarity between type name embeddings in a given embedding space $\\phi$:\n75\t\\begin{equation}\n76\t\\text{SCS}_\\phi(t_i, t_j) = \\cos(\\phi(t_i), \\phi(t_j))\n77\t\\end{equation}\n78\t\n79\tAt the batch level, Batch Confusability Score (BCS) aggregates pairwise SCS:\n80\t\\begin{equation}\n81\t\\text{BCS}(B) = \\frac{2}{|B|(|B|-1)} \\sum_{i<j} \\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)\n82\t\\end{equation}\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{SDGD: Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition}\n85\t\n86\tSDGD operationalizes confusability into decomposition. Given a confusability graph $G{=}(\\mathcal{T}, E)$ where edges connect type pairs with $\\text{SCS} > \\tau$, graph coloring partitions $\\mathcal{T}$ into $k$ batches minimizing intra-batch confusability. Three variants:\n87\t\n88\t\\begin{itemize}\n89\t\\item \\textbf{SDGD-zero}: Uses embedding SCS directly. No calibration data needed.\n90\t\\item \\textbf{SDGD-cal}: Replaces SCS with empirical confusion from 5--10 calibration examples per type.\n91\t\\item \\textbf{SDGD-v2}: Adds improved merge strategies (confidence-weighted deduplication, type-priority resolution).\n92\t\\end{itemize}\n93\t\n94\tExtraction proceeds per-batch, and results are merged via span-level deduplication.\n95\t\n96\t%--------------------------------------------------\n97\t\\section{Experiments}\n98\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n99\t%--------------------------------------------------\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQW7E4MCgex7BLGs95MZwY","session_id":"2bbd81e7-c592-4ad1-960d-e0f6cbc6cb78","uuid":"517b215d-707d-4e85-92e9-ec1a143eb013","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:46:00.670Z"}
